7592186406975 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406975 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406975 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3da669c9-a862-59b7-84e5-f7ce7ac62942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186474034 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186474035 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186474036 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ in the first quadrant?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** It is a type I region\n- **II.** It is a type II region\n- **III.** It's neither a type I nor a type II region" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d779a1b-dcae-59f0-822c-7860f9483ab7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406982 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\, & ⟹\\,x={y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/89/899357795c6d5b81d3cb619d71493ad787645697fe1f2fcecda78c1b875f388e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{x}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{3}\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406985